          %h2#Idea Idea
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phenomenology"} phenomenology
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmology"} cosmology
            %em Starobinsky model
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmic+inflation"} cosmic inflation
            takes into account – and takes as the very source of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inflaton"} inflaton
            field –
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+curvature+corrections"} higher curvature corrections
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein-Hilbert+action"} Einstein-Hilbert action
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi R
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R^2
            (square of the
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ricci+curvature"} Ricci curvature
          %p
            The Starobinsky model stands out among models of inflation as predicting a low value of the scalar-to-tensor ratio
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi r
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi r
                  %mo ∼
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 12
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi N
                        %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  r \sim \frac{12}{N^2}
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            is the number of
            = succeed "-foldings" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi e
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#KehagiasDizgahRiotto13"} Kehagias-Dizgah-Riotto 13 (2.6)
          %blockquote
            %p
              \&amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "#KalloshLinde25"}> Kallosh &amp; Linde 2025
              \&amp;rbrack;: The first realistic model of inflationary type was proposed by Starobinsky &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "#Starobinsky80"}> 1
              \&amp;rbrack;. However, it did not attempt to solve the homogeneity, isotropy, horizon, and flatness problems, which were the main goals of the inflationary scenario proposed by Guth [2]. In the beginning, the Starobinsky model stayed slightly outside the mainstream of inflationary cosmology, but then it was realized that its predictions derived by Mukhanov and Chibisov &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "#MukhanovChibisov81"}> 6
              \&amp;rbrack; provided a perfect match to the WMAP and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Planck+collaboration"} Planck
              data.
          %h2#ObservationalSupport Observational support
          %p
            Models of Starobinsky-type are favored by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/experiment"} experimental
            results (
            %a{:href => "#PlanckCollaboration13"}> PlanckCollaboration 13
            ,
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#BICEPKeckPlanck15"} BICEP2-Keck-Planck 15
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#PlanckCollaboration15"} PlanckCollaboration 15
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#BICEPKeck21"} BICEP3-Keck 18
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi r
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0.1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0.1
            (whereas other models like
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chaotic+inflation"} chaotic inflation
            are disfavored by these values), see (
            %a{:href => "#PlanckCollaboration13"}> PlanckCollaboration 13, page 12
            ).
          %p#Dat
            With respect to this data, the Starobinsky model (or “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi R
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R^2
            inflation”) is the model with the highest
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bayesian+reasoning"} Bayesian evidence
            (
            %a{:href => "#Rachen15"}> Rachen, Feb 15
            ,
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#PlanckCollaboration15XX"} PlanckCollaboration 15XX, table 6 on p. 18
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#PlanckCollaboration13"} PlanckCollaboration 13, figure 1
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Linde14"} Linde 14, figure 5
          %p#Figure1
            %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/PlanckData.jpg", :width => "800"}/
          %p
            This remains true with the data of (
            %a{:href => "#PlanckCollaboration15"}> PlanckCollaboration 15
            ), see (
            %a{:href => "#PlanckCollaboration15XIII"}> PlanckCollaboration 15 XIII, figure 22
            ) and in the final analysis (
            %a{:href => "#PlanckCollaboration18X"}> PlanckCollaboration 18X, Fig 8
            ), which gives the following (from
            = succeed "):" do
              %a{:href => "http://resonaances.blogspot.se/2015/02/weekend-plot-inflation15.html"} here
          %p#Figure2
            %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/InflationPlanck2015.jpg", :width => "800"}/
          %blockquote
            %p
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi R
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R^2
              inflation has the strongest evidence among the models considered here. However, care must be taken not to overinterpret small differences in likelihood lacking statistical significance. The models closest to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi R
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R^2
              in terms of evidence are brane inflation and exponential inflation, which have one more parameter than
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi R
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R^2
              (
              %a{:href => "#PlanckCollaboration15XX"}> PlanckCollaboration 15XX, p. 18
              )
          %p#KECKBICEPFurtherConfirmed
            This picture is further confirmed by observations of the BICEP/Keck collaboration reported in
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#BICEPKeck21"} BICEP-Keck 2021
            = succeed "):" do
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2110.00483.pdf#page=8"} Fig. 5
          %center
            %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2110.00483.pdf#page=8"}
              %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/BICEPKeck-nr-plot-211006.jpg", :width => "430"}/
          %p
            See also
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Ellis13"} Ellis 13
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Ketov13"} Ketov 13
            %a{:href => "#Efstathiou2019"} Efstathiou 2019, 50:49
            for brief survey of Starobinsky inflation in relation to observation, and see
            %a{:href => "#KehagiasDizgahRiotto13"} Kehagias-Dizgah-Riotto 13
            for more details. There it is argued that the other types of inflationary models which also reasonably fit the data are actually equivalent to the Starobinsky model during inflation.
          %h2#EmbeddingIntoSupergravity Embedding into supergravity
          %p
            Being concerned with pure
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            (the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inflaton"} inflaton
            not being an extra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/matter"} matter
            field but part of the field of
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
            (originally due to
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Cecotti87"} Cecotti 87
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#FKR13"} Farakos-Kehagias-Riotto 13
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#Ellis13"} Ellis 13
          %ul
            %li#ShrinkingOfInitialHomogeneousPatch
              %p
                shrinking the necessary initial homogeneous patch from
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∼
                      %msup
                        %mn 10
                        %mn 3
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sim 10^3
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Planck+lengths"} Planck lengths
                (which would be in need of further explanation) down to just
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∼
                      %msup
                        %mn 10
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sim 10^1
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Planck+lengths"} Planck lengths
                (
                %a{:href => "#DalianisFarakos15"}> Dalianis-Farakos 15 equations (68), (72) in v1, equations (4.11), (4.17) in v3
                , reviewed in
                = succeed ");" do
                  %a{:href => "#Dalianis16"} Dalianis 16
